"""Embedding lesson: intuition, equations, shapes, examples and guided practice."""
# title, intuition, equation, dimensions, worked example, application, explorer
TOPICS = [
('Discrete and continuous representations','A category is a name; a vector is a point with coordinates that a model can transform.',r'\mathrm{id}(w)\in\{0,\ldots,V-1\},\quad e_w\in\mathbb R^D','IDs: [B,T]; vectors: [B,T,D]','cat=2 and dog=3 are labels. Their difference 1 does not measure animal similarity.','Encode product categories or words.','Tokenization & vocabulary'),
('Categorical feature encoding','One-hot encoding assigns a separate axis to each category. ID order carries no numerical meaning.',r'x_i=\mathbf1[i=\mathrm{id}(w)]','one token: [V]','For V=4 and ID=2, x=[0,0,1,0]. Renaming IDs while permuting table rows leaves vectors unchanged.','Represent an item identity.','Matrix & one-hot'),
('One-hot and dense storage','A dense embedding has D real coordinates. Lookup avoids constructing a V-wide vector, but the table itself stores V times D parameters.',r'e=xE,\quad E\in\mathbb R^{V\times D}','x: [V]; e: [D]','V=100,D=8: float32 dense one-hot=400 bytes/token, vector=32 bytes, full table=3200 bytes; int64 ID=8 bytes. Sparse one-hot need not store 100 floats.','Compare representation and model storage separately.','Matrix & one-hot'),
('Tokenization','Split text into reusable units before mapping those units to IDs. Whitespace splitting retains punctuation; characters retain spaces.',r'\mathrm{text}\mapsto(w_1,\ldots,w_T)','sequence length T depends on tokenizer','"Cat dog" gives [cat,dog] at word level and [c,a,t,space,d,o,g] at character level.','Prepare text without downloading a model.','Tokenization & vocabulary'),
('Vocabulary and frequency','Fit a deterministic token-to-ID dictionary from a corpus. Real subword tokenizers balance word coverage and sequence length.',r'V=|\mathcal V|,\quad P=VD','table: [V,D]','[cat,dog,cat] gives counts cat=2,dog=1, plus PAD/UNK vocabulary entries.','Measure corpus coverage; train-only fitting prevents leakage.','Tokenization & vocabulary'),
('Special tokens and padding','UNK represents unseen tokens; PAD fills variable-length batches and should be masked when pooling.',r'm_{bt}=\mathbf1[X_{bt}\ne\mathrm{PAD}]','mask: [B,T]','[cat,new] with length 4 becomes [2,1,0,0]. Only two positions count in masked mean.','Batch unequal sentence lengths.','Tokenization & vocabulary'),
('Embedding matrix and lookup','A PyTorch Embedding returns the row indexed by each categorical ID.',r'e_w=E[\mathrm{id}(w),:]','E: [V,D]; input: [B,T]; output: [B,T,D]','E=[[0,0],[1,2],[3,4]], ID=2 returns [3,4]; [0,0,1]@E agrees exactly.','Learn token representations.','Matrix & one-hot'),
('Embedding dimension','D is chosen by the designer. It controls representation width and parameter count, not vocabulary size.',r'P=V D','B=2,T=5,D=3 gives output [2,5,3]','V=10,D=3 gives 30 weights. Increasing D to 6 gives 60 weights.','Explore capacity and cost.','Architecture builder'),
('Random and learned weights','Initialization supplies arbitrary coordinates. Training adjusts them for an objective; random neighbors have no semantic guarantee.',r'E^{(0)}\sim\mathcal N(0,1)','initial/trained table: [V,D]','The same seed reproduces [V,D] values; changing the seed changes random nearest neighbors.','Compare starting conditions with learned co-occurrence.','Word embedding training'),
('Gradient-based learning','A downstream loss supplies row gradients. Repeated occurrences add contributions; unused rows remain unchanged in this simple experiment.',r'E_{new}=E_{old}-\eta\nabla_E L','gradient: [V,D]','E[2]=[1,2], gradient=[0.2,-0.1], rate=0.5 gives [0.9,2.05].','Inspect credit assignment through lookup.','Gradients & updates'),
('Padding and sparse gradients','padding_idx suppresses table-row gradients from lookup. Dense gradients store a full matrix; sparse gradients store touched indices and values.',r'\nabla E_{pad}=0\quad\text{(lookup contribution)}','dense: [V,D]; sparse: selected rows','For IDs [2,2,3,0], row 2 receives two contributions, row 0 none. Weight decay or tied weights can change this behavior. SparseAdam/SGD have relevant sparse support; ordinary Adam rejects sparse gradients.','Choose compatible optimizers.','Gradients & updates'),
('Similarity metrics','Dot product depends on magnitude and direction; cosine divides out norms. Distances measure separation.',r'a^Tb=\sum_i a_i b_i,\quad \cos(a,b)=\frac{a^Tb}{\|a\|\|b\|}','two vectors: [D]','a=[1,0], b=[10,10]: dot=10, cosine≈0.7071, Euclidean≈13.4536, Manhattan=19.','Compare representations.','Similarity & retrieval'),
('Normalization and zero vectors','Unit normalization makes dot product equal cosine for nonzero vectors. Cosine is mathematically undefined at zero; the lab explicitly returns zero.',r'\hat e=e/\|e\|_2','norm: scalar per vector','[3,4] has norm 5 and unit vector [0.6,0.8]; zero remains zero under the safe convention.','Control magnitude effects in search.','Similarity & retrieval'),
('Nearest-neighbor retrieval','Score a compatible query against every candidate, sort descending and take K. Lower Euclidean/Manhattan distance instead means closer.',r'\mathrm{topK}_i\ s(q,e_i)','query: [D]; candidates: [N,D]; scores: [N]','q=[1,0], candidates [1,0] and [10,10]: cosine prefers first; raw dot prefers second.','Search and recommendation; large indexes approximate exact search.','Similarity & retrieval'),
('Word embeddings and skip-gram','Learn input and context tables by predicting nearby words against sampled alternatives.',r'L=-\log\sigma(v_w^Tu_c)-\sum_{k=1}^K\log\sigma(-v_w^Tu_{n_k})','input/context tables: [V,D]; negatives: [batch,K]','All scores 0 and K=2 gives loss 3 log(2)=2.0794. Window 1 around dog in "cat dog pet" yields dog→cat and dog→pet.','Learn corpus co-occurrence without labels.','Word embedding training'),
('Embedding space and projections','Plot D=2 or D=3 directly. PCA finds maximal-variance axes; t-SNE emphasizes local neighborhoods and may distort global distances.',r'Z=(E-\bar E)W_{PCA}','original: [V,D]; projection: [V,2] or [V,3]','Reducing D=8 to 2 discards six directions. A projected nearest point can differ from the original-space nearest neighbor.','Inspect structure while ranking in original space.','Word embedding training'),
('Sentence embeddings and pooling','Combine token vectors to a fixed width. Mask PAD positions; simple commutative pooling loses word order and is not contextual encoding.',r's=\frac{\sum_t m_t e_t}{\max(1,\sum_t m_t)}','tokens: [B,T,D]; sentence: [B,D]','[1,2] and [3,4] mean-pool to [2,3], sum to [4,6], max to [3,4]. Reversing tokens gives the same result.','A lightweight sentence baseline.','Sentence pooling'),
('Learned positional embeddings','Learn a separate table indexed by position. Adding it marks position without mixing token information.',r'z_t=e_{w_t}+p_t','token/position/combined: [B,T,D]','Token [1,2] at position vector [0.1,0.2] becomes [1.1,2.2]. Positions add max_length×D parameters.','Prepare input representations for future attention.','Positions & context'),
('Sinusoidal positions','Use fixed sine/cosine coordinates at different frequencies; no learned positional parameters.',r'PE_{p,2i}=\sin(p/10000^{2i/D}),\quad PE_{p,2i+1}=\cos(p/10000^{2i/D})','encoding: [T,D], including odd D','At position 0,D=3 the encoding is [0,1,0]. The last unpaired even coordinate still uses sine.','Explore extrapolatable position formulas, without promising model generalization.','Positions & context'),
('Static versus contextual embeddings','Static lookup gives one row per token. A contextual encoder can transform that row using surrounding words; position alone does not do so.',r'e_{cool}=E[\mathrm{id}(cool)],\quad h_t=f(e_1,\ldots,e_T)_t','static/contextual vectors: [D]','"The weather is cool." and "That movie is cool." select exactly the same cool row. Contextual representations can differ.','Understand polysemy and the motivation for attention.','Positions & context'),
('Joint embedding classification','Train embeddings and a linear classifier together on train mini-batches. Monitor validation, then inspect held-out test results.',r'z=W\operatorname{pool}(E[X])+b,\quad L=\operatorname{CE}(z,y)','W: [C,D]; logits: [B,C]; labels: [B]','V=10,D=4,C=2: 40 embedding + 8 head weights + 2 biases = 50 parameters.','Supervised text classification.','Classification laboratory'),
('Evaluation, applications and risks','Use several views: objective curves, neighbor stability, retrieval ranking, projections and downstream accuracy. Loss alone cannot establish semantic quality.',r'\mathrm{accuracy}=N^{-1}\sum_i\mathbf1[\hat y_i=y_i]','confusion matrix: [C,C]','8 correct of 10 gives 0.8 accuracy; one tiny split does not prove broad language understanding. Rare/OOV words, bias, polysemy, domain shift and language coverage remain limitations. Analogies such as king−man+woman≈queen are contingent, not guaranteed.','Evaluate search, recommendations and NLP honestly.','Evaluation & experiments'),
]

